Yes. Fear is useful when treated as information, not as sovereign command.

It can be modeled as one weighted signal among many:

\text{action} = f(\text{fear},\text{values},\text{evidence},\text{habit},\text{trust},\text{context})

The failure mode is when fear gets overweighted, generalized, or attached to the wrong cue. Then the system starts protecting against ghosts instead of actual threats. Recalibration is essentially relearning the mapping between cue and consequence.

That is where muscle memory, repetition, and embodied exposure matter. You can teach the body that a once-dangerous pattern is now safe, or that a previously ignored signal deserves attention. The useful loop is:

\text{cue} \rightarrow \text{response} \rightarrow \text{outcome} \rightarrow \text{update}

And your “us through them” frame can be powerful as empathy: temporarily model another person from inside their constraints rather than judging from outside them. But the alignment check is important—showing is different from making.

A clean version is:

\text{understand their frame}
\rightarrow
\text{offer a new relation}
\rightarrow
\text{let them test it}
\rightarrow
\text{preserve their choice}

That still allows strong guidance, teaching, coaching, ritual, story, demonstration, challenge, humor, and embodied practice. It just avoids turning insight into coercion.

So the useful principle might be:

Use fear as a sensor, trust as a stabilizer, embodiment as a calibrator, and agency as the boundary.

That gives you power without getting gunked up by the very control problem you are trying to solve.